=begin pod

=NAME Lling::Llang

=SUBTITLE Composable WFSTs and host-defined automata for Raku

=DESCRIPTION

C<Lling::Llang> provides an eager Unicode/tropical WFST builder, immutable
resource import, lazy composition, and the C<WfstProvider> role for custom Raku
automata. Provider state callbacks are cached and never execute under the
facade's cache lock.

The C<SemiringProvider> role also lets Raku define the weight algebra consumed
by native code. Arbitrary host values are represented by owned,
generation-checked tokens rather than copied object pointers.

C<DynamicLatticeValue> consumes immutable lattice values published by
LLattice or another compatible runtime. Join, meet, equality, bounded folds,
stable encodings, diagnostics, and representative-sample law validation cross
the same retained resource ABI.

=head1 Core types

=item C<WfstBuilder> constructs a dense immutable graph and is consumed by
C<build>.

=item C<ProviderArc> represents one weighted input/output transition; an
undefined label denotes epsilon.

=item C<ProviderState> represents a complete immutable state expansion.

=item C<WfstProvider> requires C<start-state> and C<state>; C<state-count> may
return the C<Int> type object when unknown.

=item C<SemiringProvider> requires C<zero>, C<one>, C<plus>, C<times>,
C<natural-order>, and C<stable-bytes>. It may implement division, Kleene star,
numeric projections, declared properties, and a closure bound.

=item C<SemiringContext> owns one retained operation context. C<SemiringWeight>
owns one provider token and supports C<clone>, C<plus>, C<times>, and
deterministic C<close>.

=item C<DynamicLatticeValue> owns one retained C<vt.lattice.val.1> value. Its
C<join>, C<meet>, C<join-many>, and C<meet-many> methods return new independent
owners.

=head1 Public operations

=item C<abi-version> and C<api-revision> report the loaded native contract.

=item C<WfstBuilder.new(:size-hint)> creates a builder. C<reserve-states>,
C<add-state>, C<set-start>, C<set-final>, C<clear-final>, and C<add-arc> mutate
it. C<build> consumes it on success; C<close> releases it without building and
C<opened> reports whether it remains usable.

=item C<resource($graph)> returns an independent retained resource.
C<import-wfst($resource)> copies a compatible immutable graph into
lling-llang. C<compose($first, $second)> returns a lazy tropical product over
independent snapshots of both inputs.

=item C<provider($implementation, :parallel, :acyclic)> exposes a
C<WfstProvider> through the shared scalar-WFST resource ABI. The optional
C<:unit-domain> and C<:weight-domain> arguments declare its data domains.

=item C<semiring-provider($implementation, :domain-id, ...)> publishes a
C<SemiringProvider>. C<:division>, C<:star>, and C<:numeric> expose optional
capabilities; C<:stable-bytes>, C<:batch>, C<:parallel>, and C<:thread-bound>
declare operational contracts.

=item C<semiring-context($resource)> validates and retains a published
semiring. Its C<zero>, C<one>, C<plus>, C<times>, C<equal>,
C<approx-equal>, C<natural-order>, optional-operation, projection,
C<stable-bytes>, and C<validate-laws> methods invoke the safe Rust adapter.

=item C<dynamic-lattice-value($resource)> validates and independently retains
a host lattice value. C<domain-id>, C<flags>, C<join>, C<meet>,
C<equivalent>, C<stable-bytes>, C<diagnostic>, C<join-many>, and C<meet-many>
expose its checked operations.

=item C<validate-lattice-laws(@values)> checks idempotence, commutativity,
associativity, and absorption over at most sixteen representative values. It
is a falsification gate, not a proof of universal laws.

=item C<provider-last-error()> copies the latest Raku callback diagnostic for
test and troubleshooting output. It is process-wide and is not a replacement
for calling a provider method directly when structured exception handling is
required.

=head1 Ownership

All returned C<Vinary::Tree::Interop::Wfst> values own one retain. Close them
deterministically. Lazy products retain independent snapshots of their inputs.
Each C<DynamicLatticeValue> and every value returned by its algebraic methods
also owns exactly one retain and must be closed exactly once.

=head1 Example

=begin code :lang<raku>
my $builder = WfstBuilder.new;
my $from = $builder.add-state;
my $to = $builder.add-state;
$builder.set-start($from).set-final($to);
$builder.add-arc($from, 'a', 'b', $to);
my $graph = $builder.build;
$graph.close;
=end code

=head1 Provider example

=begin code :lang<raku>
class RewriteAB does WfstProvider {
    method start-state(--> UInt:D) { 0 }
    method state-count(--> Int:D) { 2 }
    method state(UInt:D $id --> ProviderState:D) {
        return ProviderState.new(arcs => [ProviderArc.new(
            input => 'a'.ord, output => 'b'.ord, target => 1,
        )]) if $id == 0;
        return ProviderState.new(final => True, final-weight => 0e0)
            if $id == 1;
        ProviderState.new(valid => False)
    }
}

my $lazy = provider(RewriteAB.new, :acyclic);
$lazy.close;
=end code

=head1 Semiring provider example

=begin code :lang<raku>
class Tropical does SemiringProvider {
    method zero(--> Mu) { Inf }
    method one(--> Mu) { 0e0 }
    method plus(Mu:D $a, Mu:D $b --> Mu) { min($a, $b) }
    method times(Mu:D $a, Mu:D $b --> Mu) { $a + $b }
    method natural-order(Mu:D $a, Mu:D $b --> Int:D) {
        $a < $b ?? -1 !! $a > $b ?? 1 !! 0
    }
    method stable-bytes(Mu:D $value --> Blob:D) {
        $value.Num.Str.encode('utf8')
    }
}

my $host = semiring-provider(Tropical.new,
    domain-id => semiring-domain-id('demo.tropical.v1'), :stable-bytes);
my $algebra = semiring-context($host);
$host.close;
my $zero = $algebra.zero;
my $one = $algebra.one;
my $best = $one.plus($zero);
.close for $zero, $one, $best;
$algebra.close;
=end code

=head1 LLattice integration example

=begin code :lang<raku>
use Lling::Llang;

# `$host` is a Vinary::Tree::Interop::LatticeValue published by LLattice.
my $value = dynamic-lattice-value($host.resource);
$host.close; # `$value` owns an independent retain.
my $joined = $value.join($value);
validate-lattice-laws([$value, $joined]);
say $joined.stable-bytes.decode('utf8');
.close for $joined, $value;
=end code

=head1 Failure and concurrency contracts

Native failures throw C<X::Lling::Llang> with a stable C<Status>, operation,
and copied diagnostic. Provider exceptions are contained at the C boundary.
Customer C<state> methods execute outside the facade cache lock. Providers are
serialized unless C<:parallel> truthfully declares concurrent reentrancy.
Semiring providers default to C<:thread-bound>; their Raku operations execute
outside the recycling-arena lock. Tokens from different operation contexts
must never be mixed, even when their 16-byte domain identifiers match.
Dynamic lattice handles are also same-thread consumers. Serial callback
admission uses a nonblocking atomic gate, and the Rust consumer holds no mutex
while Raku join or meet code executes. Operands with different 16-byte lattice
domains are rejected before invoking the provider.

See the packaged C<README.md> and C<t/01-conformance.rakutest> for the complete
provider, composition, error, concurrency, and security contracts.

=end pod
